WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.000
Write a binding reaction in the direction it comes apart: the complex R–L

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.060
dissociates into free receptor and free ligand.

3
00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:17.560
The dissociation constant, Kd, is the ratio of loose to bound, and it has units of

4
00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.340
concentration. Here's the intuition that makes it usable: Kd is the free ligand

5
00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.020
concentration at which exactly half the receptor is occupied.

6
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:31.860
The scale runs backwards: a smaller Kd means tighter binding.

7
00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:38.120
The classic student error is flipping it: Kd is not Ka; lower is tighter.

8
00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.950
Everything so far assumed one site and one ligand, which gives a smooth hyperbola.

9
00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:48.890
The moment a protein has several binding sites that can sense each other, the curve

10
00:00:48.890 --> 00:00:54.310
changes shape. Hemoglobin has four sites, and binding at one changes the affinity of

11
00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.430
the others: that's cooperativity.

12
00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:02.510
The result is a sigmoid, an S-shaped curve that, as we'll see, is exactly what

13
00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:04.150
a good oxygen carrier needs.

14
00:01:05.033 --> 00:01:07.353
The Hill coefficient puts a number on the teamwork.

15
00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:13.373
Plot fractional saturation against ligand and fit it; the slope, n, tells you how

16
00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:14.653
much the sites cooperate.

17
00:01:15.373 --> 00:01:20.313
n equals 1 means every site works alone, which is myoglobin's hyperbola.

18
00:01:20.993 --> 00:01:23.613
n greater than 1 means positive cooperativity.

19
00:01:24.313 --> 00:01:30.853
Hemoglobin's n is about 2.8 out of a theoretical maximum of 4: strong cooperation,

20
00:01:31.433 --> 00:01:36.193
but not perfect. Treat the Hill equation as a black box that scores how loudly the

21
00:01:36.193 --> 00:01:37.193
subunits talk.

22
00:01:37.833 --> 00:01:42.273
2,3-BPG is the body's dial for oxygen unloading.

23
00:01:42.913 --> 00:01:48.093
It's a small, ferociously charged anion, present in red cells at nearly one-to-one

24
00:01:48.093 --> 00:01:52.873
with hemoglobin, and it wedges into a central cavity between the beta chains that

25
00:01:52.873 --> 00:01:54.813
exists only in the tense state.

26
00:01:55.273 --> 00:02:00.513
So it acts as a mechanical doorstop, stabilizing T and right-shifting the curve to

27
00:02:00.513 --> 00:02:02.213
dump more oxygen in the tissues.

28
00:02:02.213 --> 00:02:06.413
To reach the relaxed state, that BPG has to be expelled.

29
00:02:07.313 --> 00:02:11.613
Fetal hemoglobin binds it weakly, which is why the fetus runs at higher affinity.

30
00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.207
Carbon monoxide is the R-state lock.

31
00:02:15.727 --> 00:02:20.427
It binds heme far more tightly than oxygen and holds hemoglobin in the high

32
00:02:20.427 --> 00:02:26.107
-affinity relaxed state, so the oxygen still aboard refuses to release: a left

33
00:02:26.107 --> 00:02:29.407
-shifted curve and slashed carrying capacity at once.

34
00:02:30.107 --> 00:02:35.227
The blood turns cherry red, and a pulse oximeter, which can't tell CO-hemoglobin

35
00:02:35.227 --> 00:02:39.947
from oxygen hemoglobin, reads falsely normal while the tissues suffocate.

36
00:02:40.487 --> 00:02:44.407
The fix is to flood the system with oxygen and compete the CO back off.
